## ⚠️ The Foundation: Prerequisites
To successfully setup the project vending machine we need:
* GitHub Repository, Terraform Backend and Azure OIDC Integration
* GitHub Permissions
* Entra ID and Azure RBAC Permissions

### Step 1: GitHub Repository, Terraform Backend and Azure OIDC Integration
We need to simply create a new GitHub repository which will serve as our project vending machine. This will be the last repository to be created manually.
To setup the repository, the terraform backend and Azure OIDC Integration you can follow my previous [blog post](https://blog.vefiu.com/posts/terraform-github-actions/).

You need to have a working environment to deploy to Azure from your project vending machine repository.

### Step 2: GitHub Permissions

To successfully "vend" new projects by deploying GitHub repositories we must authenticate and authorize our terraform deployments against our GitHub profile. My initial aim was to avoid using PAT and instead rely on GitHub Apps. However I learned the hard way, that GitHub Apps can't be used with Personal GitHub Accounts for repository creation, but only with Organizational Accounts. Due to this, the only fallback I found was using a fine grained personal access token.

To get one of these, navigate to the settings of your GitHub Profile. On the bottom select Developer Settings and then Fine-grained tokens under Personal access tokens (PAT).

![Fine-grained tokens](../../assets/img/posts/2026-02-github-management-iac/ghm-personal-1.png)

Now give your token a name, set the expiration period, and make sure it can access all repositories. Then add the following permissions:
* Administration: Read and write
* Contents: Read and write
* Secrets: Read and write
* Metadata will be added automatically

![Fine-grained tokens](../../assets/img/posts/2026-02-github-management-iac/ghm-personal-2.png)

Now click on generate token and copy your token. Make sure you protect it adequately.

### Step 3: Entra ID and Azure RBAC Permissions
To successfully "vend" identities to other projects and be able to deploy to a scope in Azure (as example a subscription), we need to grant our in [step 1](### Step 1: GitHub Repository, Terraform Backend and Azure OIDC Integration) created central Service Principal additional privileges within Entra ID and Azure.

| Scope                                                  | Role Required                 | Why?                                                                           |
| :----------------------------------------------------- | :---------------------------- | :----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Entra ID**                                           | **Application Developer**     | To create App Registrations and configure Federated Credentials for new repos. |
| **Azure (e. g. Management Group, Subscription, etc.)** | **User Access Administrator** | To assign the 'Contributor' role to the newly created project identities.      |

> **Tip:** Start with `Application Developer`, but if your Terraform plan fails if you want to "Patch" an existing SPN, upgrade to `Application Administrator` to allow full lifecycle management of the Entra ID objects.

