## 📂 Repository Structure: The Framework Layout

The project vending machine repository is structured as follows

```
project-vending-machine/
├── .github/
│   └── workflows/
│       └── create-project.yml     # CI/CD Pipeline for our project vending
├── modules/
│   ├── entra-spn/                 # Module: Vends SPNs & OIDC Trust
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   └── repository/                # Module: Standardizes GitHub Repos & creates secrets
│       ├── main.tf
│       ├── variables.tf
│       └── outputs.tf
├── projects/
│   ├── main.tf                    # The "Orchestrator" (Calls modules)
│   ├── providers.tf               # Provider & Backend config
│   └── variables.tf               # Sensitive credential mapping
├── .gitignore                     # Prevents local .tfvars/certs leaks
└── README.md                      # Documentation
```

## 🔍 Structure Breakdown
* `modules/entra-spn`: This is used for create Azure SPNs. It is decoupled from GitHub so we could technically use it to vend identities for other platforms in the future.
* `modules/repository`: This is our Module to create our projects in GitHub. It doesn't just create a repo; it "decorates" it with the secrets provided by the Identity module and enforces our security baseline (Secret Scanning, Branch Protection).
* `projects/`: This is our Project Vending. By keeping this separate from the modules, we could technically manage different "stacks" (e.g., a production folder for our blog projects and a lab folder for temporary experiments) while using the same underlying logic.

By modularizing the Identity and Repository logic, adding a new project to my portfolio requires zero 'copy-pasting' of code. I simply declare a new module block in projects/main.tf, and the entire project skeleton — from Entra ID to GitHub Secrets — is provisioned automatically. Through the use of modules we additional ensure consistent settings across the environments and we also ensure that every project gets its own `.tfstate` file in Azure, reducing the blast radius of any potential state corruption.

